# **This tree's line ending is LF, in the working copy as well as in the object
# store, on every machine that checks it out.**
#
# Not a style preference — a correctness rule this repository leans on in three
# different ways, all of which a CRLF checkout breaks silently:
#
#   * Dozens of tests read *this source* as text and assert on its shape:
#     `include_str!("main.rs")` and friends look for `"\n    }\n"` to find where a
#     method ends, for `"\nstruct App {\n"` to find a declaration. Under CRLF none
#     of those needles is in the haystack, and the failure is a `panic!` about
#     braces rather than anything about line endings — 67 of them, in one run.
#   * Fixtures are compared byte for byte against what the product writes:
#     `assets/schemes/folio-dark.json` against `bt_persist::write_scheme`,
#     `docs/plans/attention/codex-notify.toml` against the line the codex
#     installer adds. Folio writes LF; a fixture smudged to CRLF on checkout is a
#     fixture that no longer says what it was written to say.
#   * `scripts/shell-integration/*.bash` is read by POSIX shells, which take a CR
#     as part of the command.
#
# Git for Windows installs `core.autocrlf=true` by default and the GitHub Windows
# runners keep it, so this is the difference between "passes here" and "passes on
# a machine nobody configured". `eol=lf` states it in the repository instead of
# asking every clone to be set up the same way.
#
# Order matters below: `.gitattributes` is last-match-wins, so this general line
# comes first and every exception to it comes after.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The bash integration script is read by POSIX shells and must keep LF endings
# whatever `core.autocrlf` is set to — see `shell_integration::tests`. Implied by
# the line above now; kept because it is the case that taught us the rule, and
# because it should survive any future narrowing of that line.
*.bash text eol=lf

# The ref recordings are raw terminal byte streams. CR is data in them, not a line
# ending: eight of them were committed with their CRs stripped, and the ref tests
# failed on every fresh checkout while the tree they were normalised in kept passing.
*.recording binary

# The corpus recordings are the same kind of file and the same failure waiting to
# happen. Git currently treats them as binary only because it happens to find a NUL
# byte in the first 8000 of them — a guess, not a rule, and a recording that begins
# with 8000 bytes of printable output would be normalised and silently corrupted.
# Saying it outright also keeps `git grep` honest about them: a file Git calls
# binary is a file `git grep` skips, and these are the ones worth searching.
*.btcr binary

# ── bytes that are not ours to normalise ──────────────────────────────────────
#
# Everything below was committed with CRLF because that is how its author wrote
# it, and each is a file whose *bytes* are the point. `-text` — not `binary`, so
# `git grep` and diffs still read them — turns off every conversion in both
# directions: they check out as they were committed, and `git status` does not
# report them as modified for a difference this file would otherwise introduce.

# Microsoft's PSReadLine 2.4.6, shipped verbatim. `bt_app::psreadline` carries
# `PSReadLine.psm1` in the executable with `include_bytes!` and compares the
# installed copy against it; a checkout that rewrote its line endings would make
# the shipped module and the repaired one different files.
assets/psreadline/** -text

# The upstream notices Apache-2.0 §4(b) requires to be reproduced. A notice is
# reproduced or it is not.
licenses/** -text

# Captured evidence and the spike probes: transcripts of what a tool printed and
# source that was written and run outside this workspace. Both are records of
# something that happened, and a record is not edited to match house style.
docs/plans/attention/evidence/** -text
docs/plans/web-preview/codex-final-pass.md -text
docs/spikes/artifacts/win-landing/probes/** -text
